[04:17.10]Conversation Two
[04:19.11]M: You say your shop has been doing well. 
[04:22.04]Could you give me some idea of what ¡°doing well¡± means in facts and figures?
[04:27.27]W: Well, ¡°doing well¡± means averaging ¡ê1,200 or more a week for about seven years, making almost a quarter of a million pounds. 
[04:39.19]And ¡°doing well¡± means your earnings are rising. 
[04:43.31]Last year, we did slightly over 50,000 and this year we hope to do more than 60,000. 
[04:50.74]So that's good, if we continue to rise.
[04:54.00]M: Now, that's gross earnings, I assume. 
[04:56.93]What about your expenses?
[04:59.36]W: Yes, that's gross. 
[05:01.07]The expenses, of course, go up steadily. 
[05:04.39]And since we've moved to this new shop, the expenses have increased greatly, because it's a much bigger shop. 
[05:12.28]So I couldn't say exactly what our expenses are. 
[05:16.62]They are something in the region of six or seven thousand pounds a year, which is not high.
[05:23.31]Commercially speaking, it's fairly low, and we try to keep our expenses as low as we can.
[05:29.91]M: And your prices are much lower than the same goods in shops round about. 
[05:35.03]How do the local shopkeepers feel about having a shop doing so well in their midst?
[05:40.82]W: Perhaps a lot of them don't realize how well we are doing, because we don't make a point of publicizing. 
[05:47.62]That was a lesson we learned very early on. 
[05:51.26]We were very friendly with all local shopkeepers and we happened to mention to a local shopkeeper how much we had made that week. 
[06:00.00]He was very unhappy and never as friendly again. 
[06:04.78]So we make a point of never publicizing the amount of money we make. 
[06:09.74]But we are on very good terms with all the shops. 
[06:13.17]None of them have ever complained that we are putting them out of business or anything like that.
[06:18.81]I think it's a nice friendly relationship. 
[06:22.25]Maybe if they did know what we made, perhaps they wouldn't be so friendly.
